  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1232 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nDrier conditions are spreading across the area as the winter system \nmoves off to the east. Brief shortwave ridging take over tonight \ninto tomorrow allowing temperatures to warm into the 50s this \nweekend. Behind this ridge, a longwave digging trof over the Plains \nproduces a lee low at the sfc that swings through OK, northern \nAR, and is into SEMO by Sunday morning. This fast moving system \ncould start to bring showers back to the Quad State as soon as \nlate tomorrow night and into Sunday morning.\n\nAs this upper trof and sfc low swing through the Quad State, precip \nwill increase from west to east ahead of the associated cold front. \nAlthough instability is minimal (less than 300 J/kg of MUCAPE, and \ndew points are in the upper 40s to low 50s) mid-level lapse rates \nlook to be around 6-7.5 degC/km and bulk shear is around 45-50 kts. \nThis would be sufficient for some thunderstorm development among the \nshowers. NBM precip totals range from 0.10\" southwest to around \n0.50\" northeast. This system will also bring breezy winds area wide \nSunday afternoon with the cold front. Right now sustained winds look \nto be around 15-25 mph however, models don't entirely agree on the \nstrength or placement of the sfc low. Should the low deepen, winds \ncould be stronger.  \n\nBehind this system sfc high pressure takes over and brings a warming \ntrend for next week. Highs reach back up into the 60s by mid to late \nweek. Conditions remain dry next week until potentially Thursday \nafternoon/evening when 50-70% chances for precip return to the \nforecast thanks to the approach of an upper level closed low.",
